Ramesh a 27 year old farmer in India  lives happily with his family with his  wife and two kids every day morning he  goes to his farm works hard grows his  crops comes back tired and exhausted and  evening to have a meal with his family  and on one day when his wife could not  find him back home she took the kids and  went out searching for him she found him  hanging dead on a tree above his farm  Ramesh committed suicide because he  could not repay a $200 loan that he took  from a microfinance Institute I did not  know Ramesh but as I sat in my high-rise  apartment in Abu Dhabi having built a  career in financial services I felt I  had betrayed I had betrayed my  grandfather and generations before him  who were farmers I felt I had betrayed  the community and small village in India  that I come from in 2006 when Nobel  Peace Prize was given to Muhammad Yunus  for microfinance I was excited and happy  because it was considered the ultimate  financial solution that would help the  poor foreign aid organisations wealthy  philanthropists and global corporations  invested millions of dollars into  microfinance even us who had a few  dollars to spare went onto websites like  Kiva and on click of a mouse lend money  to form  in Ecuador microfinance appealed to the  emotions of a lot of people because of  the simple model it would lend tiny  loans to the poor who could invest grow  business generate returns and then climb  out of poverty today ten years after the  Nobel Peace Prize 40 years since  microfinance has started there's little  evidence that microfinance helps the  poor on the contrary there are several  studies that shows it does not a study  done here at Stanford University across  87 villages in Bangladesh found  microfinance to have no impact on the  income of the poor and in certain cases  found it to have a negative impact a  study done at MIT across 52  neighborhoods in Hyderabad in India  found microfinance did not have any  impact on health education or income of  the poor people and finally a study done  at Yale University in Philippines found  no impact on income of the poor due to  microfinance microfinance as it stands  on a shaky foundation due to three main  reasons first it considers every poor  person to be an entrepreneur you give  him capital he could invest and grow a  business which is in fact not true  studies have found that less than 2% of  micro enterprises survive for more than  three years  number two the high interest rates I was  speaking to a CEO of a microfinance firm  recently and one of the first questions  I asked him was what's your interest  rate  obviously did not give me an answer but  he explained how it works and this is  what he said we give a loan of $10 for  two weeks and expect $12 in return if  you do the math like I did that works  out to 20% interest for two weeks if you  compute that on an annual basis it works  to thousands of percentage in interest  thousands of percentage in interest  every poor person has to pay  microfinance justifies the saying it's  to cover the cost of operation it is to  cover the risk of the business forbes  magazine published a list of top 50  microfinance institutes on an average it  found these firms generating 80% return  on capital 80 percent return there are  very few business in the world which  generates 80 percent annually on capital  and finally the abusive collection  practices microfinance claims 95 percent  collection rate but how do they achieve  this Khurshid Allah was a debt collector  for Grameen Bank in Bangladesh he was  asked to retire early because he  questioned their collection practices  they were asked to threaten and abuse  poor borrowers who are not able to repay  poor borrowers were asked to sell their  clothes or even sell their children to  repay the loan microfinance  started off with the best of intentions  but has failed to deliver on the promise  to the poor and as I did my research  even I failed to find a silver bullet  solution for the problems of the poor  problems of the poor are big complain  and challenging today remains wife faces  the challenge of feeding her young  children at least one meal a day  millions of people across the globe face  similar challenges and tough choices  each and every day of their lives and  what they need is not a microfinance  loan that could make their life  difficult what they need is a regular  source of income that could make their  life easier I am in quest for searching  for a solution and I need your help and  support to find the solution I've  launched this website which is I pledged  world.com to source solutions that could  help solve the problems of the poor so  let us take this first step together to  change the lives of the poor people to  change the way organizations interact  with the poor and the dream of a changed  world that would have a better tomorrow  thank you very much  you
 

